From f09a98abc6cf6a4a1bde9d30b85e9770cf0cf4e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 05:33:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Follow the scroll with the A-Z strip

The strip marked where the listing began, which stopped being the answer as soon as
you scrolled: browsing from K into L left K lit.

Every rendered item now carries the bucket it is filed under, and a scroll handler
asks the document what is sitting just under the strip. One hit test per frame,
and it does not care that list view recycles its rows while panel view does not -
it reads what is actually on screen rather than what the listing believes it drew.

Which bucket an item belongs to is worked out in the frontend rather than asked
for, because it is only used to light up a letter. bucketOf folds accents with NFD,
which covers the names that actually occur; the transliterations the server also
does (AE for the ligature, R for the Cyrillic one) are rare enough that a briefly
wrong highlight costs nothing, and they are wrong only in the highlight, never in
where a jump lands.

Picking a letter still marks it immediately - the reading is cleared on a jump, so
the strip follows the click until the next scroll has something to say.

bucketOf is unit-tested. The probe around it is not: jsdom has no layout, so
elementFromPoint returns nothing there, and the listing's harness does not render
the scroller the items live in.

